The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
pdev = of_find_device_by_node(node);
if (!pdev) {
    ...
    ret = -ENODEV;
    goto put_node;
}
```
Reason:
The absence of a `put_device()` call when `of_find_device_by_node()` succeeds means that the reference to the device is not released properly. If the function exits due to an error after successfully finding the device, it can lead to memory leaks or dangling pointers since the reference count for the device is not decremented.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
put_device:
    put_device(&pdev->dev);
```
Reason:
Adding the `put_device(&pdev->dev);` call ensures that the device reference is properly released when the function exits, regardless of whether it exits due to an error or successfully. This prevents memory leaks and ensures proper resource management.

